From 77c01c5c6fa72ae3fd3e4a97b65e61fbfa0b47d5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ajensenwaud <ajensenwaud@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 19:33:02 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 36/60] MiMo-V2.6-Flash on CUDA: EXL3, DFlash, two GPUs

The mimo_v2 family serves an EXL3 pack of MiMo-V2.6-Flash on the native CUDA server, on one GPU or two DGX Sparks
as tensor-parallel ranks, with the pack's DFlash drafter.

The port follows Jay Leaton's DeepSeek-V4.1 method: each launch mirrors the Python 0.6 engine's binding, Triton
kernels run as the Python engine's own captured cubins (TENSORFOLD_CUDA_KERNELS), and the ranks run over his
tensor-parallel session and RoCE mailbox.

- cuda/exl3.zig launches copies of the Python engine's EXL3 kernels (linear, unpack, routed and grouped experts) as
  its bindings do; the copies keep their source blob in the header.
- families/mimo_v2: config and rank cuts, loading, the decode forward (eager and graphed by rows and key bucket),
  the prompt path (prompt GEMM, full-attention prompt kernel, experts grouped up to 3,072 rows a chunk in 1,024-row
  windows, per expert beyond), the DFlash drafter, and the engine both ranks run.
- native.zig: the lane backend. Rank 0 serves and sends every GPU step over the plan link before launching it; rank
  1 runs `tf-mimo follow`. --parallel sets the streams' cache slots. Prompts under 256 tokens run as decode rows; a
  lone stream's first copy window is 6 rows; draft depth uses the lanes' geometric rule with a context term.
- native/cuda.zig: MiMo registered; a tensor-parallel family is admitted on its rank's share of the weights, and a
  family without prompt-state copies serves without the prompt cache.
- Checks: tf-cuda-test mimo-exl3 and mimo-exl3-fixtures (against the Python engine's bytes, with the oracle
  script), tf-mimo m1 (residuals, logits and argmax byte-equal to the Python engine), host tests for config, cuts,
  prompt plans and the hello.

(cherry picked from commit 51db34afe840a539b763f15a7808136cca9c2301)
